        "mode": "montage"
      },
      "strings": {
        "stepAway": "Look up from the kiosk and carry on in the room for a minute. Someone may ask you something. Your place is saved.",
        "worldBeatHeading": "Try the attacker's side",
        "worldBeat": "Nilesh has something for you. Look up from the kiosk; your place is saved.",
        "worldBeatWaiting": "Still out there. Carry on whenever you like.",
        "weakestTells": "The risks most often missed: {names} ({counts} each).",
        "weakestTell": "The risk most often missed: {names} ({counts}).",
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Tarrowby Logistics, a fictional firm. This one is about shadow IT: staff using devices, apps, accounts or services that IT doesn't know about or hasn't approved, usually as a shortcut. Client files are due by Monday and the file share has slowed to a crawl. First you make the calls anyone would under a deadline, then see, six months on, where each shortcut led. In between you can try the attacker's side, leaving an unlabelled USB stick where staff will find it. Then you sort an inbox of USB sticks that turned up and 'quicker way' messages, and match each shortcut to the approved way. Only the inbox is scored.",
        "flipHeading": "Six months later",
        "allRight": "You took the safe route on every one of these. Nothing to pick over here.",
        "allPhishRight": "Every risky shortcut was stopped, with every reason named.",
        "noBlameLine": "Shortcuts are well meant, usually taken under a deadline. Being caught by one is no fault of yours; Colin was right that the share was slow. The move is to tell IT so the data gets somewhere safe.",
        "noBlameLineCritical": "These shortcuts are tempting when a deadline bites, so a miss here says more about the pressure than about you. The points only show where to look next time.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: pick the shortcut you reach for most, and learn the approved swap for it. Work stays in work places, not your personal email, personal cloud or a free online service. A USB stick or other device IT hasn't checked, whether found, posted, handed over or given away, goes to IT unplugged. If the official way is slow, tell IT rather than build your own way round, and if you're unsure about something, report it to IT. If personal data went to a personal account, the data protection officer needs to know too.",
        "habitLine": "When the official way is too slow, tell IT rather than build your own way round it.",
        "defendIntro": "Now go through the USB sticks that turned up and the 'quicker way' messages and decide what to do with each. Some are safe to act on and some aren't. There's no clock on you here.",
        "defendOnly": "Every shortcut here is the kind a busy person takes with good intentions, and every one has a quiet cost. The ones you were caught out by are shown below, with the approved way.",
        "teamLine": "By reporting or checking {n} of the risky ones rather than acting on them, you kept an unknown USB stick or a risky shortcut away from the rest of your team.",
        "conceptPanel": [
          {
            "heading": "Shadow IT, and those USB sticks",
            "text": "Shadow IT is staff using devices, apps, accounts or services that IT doesn't know about or hasn't approved, usually as a shortcut under a deadline. Emailing client files to a personal address, running the rota in a free app, passing addresses round a personal group chat and plugging a visitor's USB stick into a work laptop are all shadow IT. So is any device IT hasn't checked, down to a USB stick.\n\nAn unknown USB stick can run code the moment it's plugged in, before anyone opens a file. That holds whether it was found on the floor, posted in, handed over by a visitor or given away at a stand. The unlabelled one in Nilesh's drop test was ours: a harmless tracker IT knew about, which only reports that it was plugged in, and we name nobody. Most people who plug a found stick in are being kind, trying to find the owner, or just curious, so the kind move is to hand it to IT unplugged.\n\nTo a real attacker, the shortcut and the kindness are both openings. Whoever leaves a stick to be found gets a way onto a work machine. Whoever breaks into a personal inbox, or runs the free service the client files went to, gets a copy of a whole logistics client's consignment file: names, addresses and delivery schedules that nobody can account for. Handing unknown devices to IT and keeping files in approved tools closes both gaps.\n\nSix months on it surfaces: a service nobody can reach still holds the client files with no contract behind it, or a machine has to be wiped and rebuilt, and the clean-up means finding every copy, proving deletion and answering the client."
          },
          {
            "heading": "The found USB stick: handed to IT",
            "text": "Nilesh checked it on a machine wired to nothing. A stick left to be found is often set to run the second it's plugged in. On a work machine, that's someone else's way in, which is why it never goes near one until it's been checked.",
            "condition": "globalVars.kit_ca15_stick_choice === 'handin'"
          },
          {
            "heading": "The found USB stick: into lost property",
            "text": "The stick sat in lost property until the next day, when it reached IT unchecked. Anyone looking for its owner could have plugged it in first.",
            "condition": "globalVars.kit_ca15_stick_choice === 'lost'"
          },
          {
            "heading": "The found USB stick: left on the floor",
            "text": "The stick lay on the floor until Chidi picked it up. Whoever left it there only needs one kind or curious person to plug it in.",
            "condition": "globalVars.kit_ca15_stick_choice === 'leave'"
          }
        ],
        "flipMontageByChoice": {
          "personal_email": {
            "send_personal": "Months on, the personal inbox was broken into. The client files were still in its sent items, and IT had never known the copy was there, so nobody could protect it or take it back. The client got a convincing fake invoice built from them.",
            "ask_nilesh": "Through remote access the client files never left the firm, so there was nothing outside to find. In the version where they went to a personal inbox, that inbox was broken into, and IT never knew a copy was there to protect."
          },
          "drivers_chat": {
            "add_to_group": "The chat grew to forty people, two of them long gone from the firm. A photo of a delivery note with a customer's home address was passed on outside it.",
            "ask_channel": "IT set up a team channel within a day, and anyone who left lost access with their account. The version where the personal group chat stayed in use had a delivery note photo passed on outside the firm."
          },
          "visitor_stick": {
            "plug_visitor": "The surveyor's USB stick had been in three other firms' laptops first. It carried a program that ran on yours and copied the folders it could reach, and Tarrowby had to tell two clients.",
            "send_link": "The drawings came through the firm's link and were checked on the way in, so nothing arrived on a stick. The version where the stick went into the laptop carried a program that copied the folders it could reach."
          },
          "rota_app": {
            "use_app": "The rota stayed editable by the departed temp, then vanished when the free app closed the unused account, the week before a busy run.",
            "ask_it": "IT moved the rota to the team space in minutes. The version where it stayed in the free app could still be edited by the departed temp, and was lost when the account was closed."
          }
        }
      },
